{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize drugs will end on Sunday when a new law takes over, making it illegal again to carry small quantities of hard substances. The updated legislation, HB4002, gives individuals caught with drugs like fentanyl, heroin, or meth two options: face charges for possession or enter a treatment program. This includes finishing a behavioral health course and joining a deflection initiative to dodge fines. Holding personal-use amounts will now be a misdemeanor, carrying a possible six-month jail term. The law also strengthens penalties for distributing drugs near locations such as parks and focuses on public drug use enforcement.\n\nThe new rule encourages, though doesn’t enforce, counties to build alternatives to steer people away from legal repercussions and toward support for addiction and mental health. Supporters argue this lets areas tailor programs to their means, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent and unfair policies. “Eligibility for deflection is tight: no other offenses, no outstanding warrants, no violent acts, and stable health,” said Portland’s top police official, Bob Day. If someone qualifies, officers contact the county’s deflection dispatch to verify details and link them to a mobile health team, which has just 30 minutes to respond. “If the team can’t arrive in that time, the person goes to jail. We can’t just delay,” Day explained.\n\n***\n\nThe shift back to criminalization marks the close of Oregon’s initial attempt at drug decriminalization, which began years ago. The new law aims to balance enforcement with access to care, though it leaves much up to local resources. Critics argue the narrow deflection criteria could leave many without help, while supporters believe it offers a practical middle ground between punishment and treatment. The 30-minute response window for health teams adds urgency, ensuring quick decisions but risking rushed outcomes.\n\nThe law’s passage reflects ongoing debates about how best to handle substance use, with opinions divided on whether harsher penalties or expanded support will yield better results. As counties adapt, the success of the approach may hinge on funding, infrastructure, and community buy-in.\n\nP.P.S ['age', 'country', 'eat', 'shopping', 'tongue']", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.0026003853417932987}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.14652255177497864}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.6823751330375671}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.9492557644844055}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0014953156933188438}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.03065778873860836}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -1.2516897186287679e-05}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.4638240878121e-06}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -0.24465131759643555}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.8567045331001282}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.7580496072769165}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -0.44483089447021484}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -6.0794889577664435e-05}, {"token": " when", "logprob": -1.4701495170593262}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0072907814756035805}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -1.0550663471221924}, {"token": " law", "logprob": -1.064085602760315}, {"token": " takes", "logprob": -0.5308147072792053}, {"token": " over", "logprob": -0.8973615765571594}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.01898849941790104}, {"token": " making", "logprob": -0.45730823278427124}, {"token": " it", "logprob": -0.8496440649032593}, {"token": " illegal", "logprob": -0.011920408345758915}, {"token": " again", "logprob": -0.13469088077545166}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0005595310358330607}, {"token": " carry", "logprob": -0.8074946999549866}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.010743869468569756}, 